knows	two	knows	P3	the man knows fully the answer to what he would enquire about	the man does not know even the subject, so he cannot say what he is looking for
can_enquire	two	enquire	P2	he is able to enquire about it at all	there is some need or point in his enquiring about it
# For "knows": the dilemma (P1) needs knowing and not knowing to exhaust the cases. But in P3, not knowing means having no grasp even of the subject. The denial of P2's full knowledge allows partial knowledge, which falls under neither branch.
# For "can_enquire": S2 supports the knower's branch only with "he has no need to enquire", yet C states it as "cannot enquire". I was unsure which statements P2 and P3 are, since they are not quoted. I assigned the "no need" sense to P2, the knower's branch.
